> The right of first refusal refers to a tenants right to move back in once
> the renovations are complete. It does not imply any right to refuse the
> renovations.
>
> If you refuse to move out based on the notice the landlord has the right
> to file for an Order of Possession.
>
> Assuming that the landlord had followed the procedure correctly (using
> proper form, following proper timeline) it could be a very easy win for
> them if they pursued an eviction against you, in such a situation.
>
> If the landlord does not do the renovations within six months of the
> tenant moving out, the tenant can come back and make a claim against the
> landlord on the basis that they gave the notice in bad faith. This can
> include claiming for the difference of your rent for up to twelve months.
>
> The landlord has no obligation to provide you with an alternative
> apartment while they are doing the renovations.
>
> The landlord must provide a breakdown of the renovations they intend to d=
o
> and an estimate of what the rent will be when the renovations are complet=
e.
>
>
>
> RTB/SS
